Fork notes (kernicek/ptouch-print):
-----------------------------------

Forked from https://github.com/enachb/ptouch-print (`upstream` remote) to
drive a PT-P710BT for batch label printing.

Stock ptouch-print always auto-cuts the tape (ptouch_eject()) at the end of
every invocation, even though everything given to one invocation (multiple
--image/--cutmark) already prints as a single continuous job. That's fine
for one label, but printing several labels as SEPARATE ptouch-print calls
(e.g. one per run of a script, over time, rather than all batched into one
command) meant every single call fed and cut its own tape, wasting a
feed-to-cutter margin of tape on every call.

Added `--final-cut`: printing now defaults to feed-without-cut
(ptouch_ff()) at the end instead of always cutting, so the tape stays
attached to the roll and the *next* ptouch-print invocation's content
prints immediately after it with zero wasted margin in between. Pass
`--final-cut` when you actually want to cut the tape free.

Build note: the po/ (gettext translations) subdirectory's generated
Makefile has no `all` target with this repo's autotools setup on a current
Fedora toolchain, so `./autogen.sh && ./configure && make` fails in `po/`.
Not needed for this fork's use case (no translations), so it's compiled
directly instead of via the full autotools build:

    gcc -O2 -Iinclude -I. -DHAVE_CONFIG_H -DLOCALEDIR='""' -include locale.h \
      src/libptouch.c src/ptouch-print.c \
      $(pkg-config --cflags --libs libusb-1.0 gdlib) \
      -o ptouch-print

(`./configure` still needs to have been run once, to generate `config.h`.)
